        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.swampscottma.gov/1568/History-of-Swampscott",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early Native Americans called the area the “land of the red rock,” a name that echoes in Swampscott’s geography today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.swampscottma.gov/1568/History-of-Swampscott",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town officially separated from Lynn and incorporated on May 21, 1852, after a smooth legislative vote.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.swampscottma.gov/1568/History-of-Swampscott",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Swampscott’s historic 1700s saltbox home, once owned by John Humphrey, now houses the Swampscott Historical Society at 99 Paradise Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.swampscottma.gov/1568/History-of-Swampscott",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "An abandoned 4‑mile Boston & Maine Railroad branch in Swampscott now powers the Marblehead Rail Trail, a favorite for cyclists.",
